{{wikipedia}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|sa}} {{term|sc=Deva|देवनागरी|tr=Devánāgarī}}, compound of {{term|sc=Deva|देव|tr=devá||divine|lang=sa}} + {{term|sc=Deva|नगर|tr=nágara|lang=sa||city}}; literally “divine city writing”.<!--The name Devanagari should therefore be understood as “excellent or perfected form of the Nagari script”.-->

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˌdeɪvəˈnɑːɡəriː/}}
{{rfap}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Of the Devanagari script or of a Devanagari alphabet.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# An [[abugida]] script used to write several [[Indian]] languages, mainly [[Sanskrit]] and [[Hindi]], but also [[Marathi]], [[Kashmiri]], [[Sindhi]], [[Bihari]], [[Bhili]], [[Konkani]], and [[Bhojpuri]]. It is also used to write [[Nepali]] in [[Nepal]].

